DNS — Domain Name System

DNS translates a human-readable domain name into an IP address.

For example:

www.google.com
       ↓
DNS
       ↓
142.250.x.x

Instead of remembering an IP address, we use google.com.

DNS only resolves name → IP. After that, the browser opens TCP/TLS and makes the HTTP request.


How DNS works — step by step

Suppose you enter:

https://api.example.com/users

Your browser needs the IP of api.example.com.

1. Browser cache
   Checks if it already knows the IP.

2. OS cache
   If not, OS DNS cache is checked.

3. DNS Resolver (recursive resolver)
   Request goes to ISP resolver or public one (e.g. Google 8.8.8.8, Cloudflare 1.1.1.1).
   This resolver does the heavy lifting for the client.

Client
  ↓
DNS Resolver

4. Root DNS Server
   Resolver asks root: who handles .com?
   Root: ask the .com TLD servers.
   >>>>>>>>>>>>>>  TLD = the LAST part of a domain name (rightmost label) — the “branch” under the DNS root. e.g  google.com     → TLD = .com


5. TLD Server (.com)
   Points to authoritative nameservers for example.com.

.com
 ↓
Authoritative DNS for example.com

6. Authoritative DNS Server
   Holds the real records for the zone:

api.example.com → 192.168.10.50

7. Resolver returns the IP (and caches it per TTL)
>>>>>>>>>>>> TTL = how long (in seconds) a DNS answer may be CACHED before asking again.

Browser
   ↓
192.168.10.50
   ↓
TCP + TLS + HTTPS to that IP

Resolver vs Authoritative (interview):
  Recursive resolver → finds the answer on behalf of the client (asks around, caches)
  Authoritative server → source of truth for that domain’s records


═══════════════════════════════════════
What is TLD? (Top-Level Domain)
═══════════════════════════════════════

TLD = the LAST part of a domain name (rightmost label) — the “branch” under the DNS root.

Examples:
  google.com     → TLD = .com
  example.org    → TLD = .org
  shop.pk        → TLD = .pk

Break a full name (right → left):

  api . example . com
   │       │       │
   │       │       └── TLD (Top-Level Domain)
   │       └── second-level domain (registered name)
   └── subdomain

In the lookup chain, root does NOT know example.com’s IP.
Root only says: “for .com, go ask the .com TLD servers.”
TLD servers then point to the authoritative nameservers for example.com.

Types (quick):
  gTLD  — generic: .com, .org, .net, .app
  ccTLD — country: .pk, .uk, .de

One-liner: TLD = suffix like .com — which top branch of DNS handles this name.


═══════════════════════════════════════
What is TTL? (Time To Live)
═══════════════════════════════════════

TTL = how long (in seconds) a DNS answer may be CACHED before asking again.

TLD ≠ TTL  (easy mix-up!)
  TLD → part of the domain name hierarchy (.com)
  TTL → cache lifetime of a DNS record (300 seconds, etc.)


Important DNS record types

| Record   | Purpose                    | Example                      |
| -------- | -------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| **A**    | Domain → IPv4              | example.com → 1.2.3.4        |
| **AAAA** | Domain → IPv6              | example.com → 2001:…         |
| **CNAME**| Alias → another name       | www → example.com            |
| **MX**   | Mail servers               | example.com → mail provider  |
| **TXT**  | Text / verification         | SPF, DKIM, domain verify     |
| **NS**   | Who is authoritative       | ns1.example.com              |
| **PTR**  | IP → domain (reverse DNS)  | used in reverse lookups      |
| **SRV**  | Service host + port        | _sip._tcp.example.com        |


Very important for interviews — A vs CNAME

A:
  api.example.com → 10.10.10.5     (direct to IP)

CNAME:
  www.example.com → example.com    (alias to another name; then that name’s A/AAAA is used)

Rules of thumb:
- A/AAAA → IP
- CNAME → another domain name (not an IP)
- Apex/root domain (example.com) usually should be A/AAAA (or ALIAS/ANAME at some providers),
  not a normal CNAME, because CNAME at apex conflicts with other required records (MX, NS, …)


What is TTL? (details + example)

TTL = Time To Live — how long resolvers may cache the answer.

api.example.com
A → 10.10.10.5
TTL = 300          → cache up to 5 minutes

Dry run (samajhne ke liye):
  0:00  resolver learns IP 10.10.10.5 (TTL 300)
  0:01–4:59  same question → answer from CACHE (no root/TLD trip)
  5:00  cache expired → resolver asks again

High TTL (e.g. 3600):
  ✅ fewer DNS queries
  ❌ IP change / failover slow to propagate

Low TTL (e.g. 60):
  ✅ users pick up new IP faster
  ❌ more DNS lookups

Migration tip:
  Lower TTL ahead of a cutover (e.g. to 60s), wait for old TTL to expire,
  then change IP — users flip faster.

One-liner: TTL = how long resolvers may remember the IP.


Extra interview bits
- Multiple A records → simple DNS load distribution / failover (client picks one)
- DNS does not replace a load balancer for smart health-based routing by itself
- Private DNS inside VPC/cluster for internal service names


DNS in a microservices architecture

Instead of hardcoding:

http://10.10.20.15:8080

use a name:

http://payment-service:8080

DNS / service discovery resolves:

payment-service → 10.10.20.15 (or a virtual Service IP)

Kubernetes example:

payment-service.default.svc.cluster.local
  → ClusterIP of the Service

So DNS is often part of service discovery (CoreDNS in K8s).


Interview answer

"DNS resolves human-readable names to IP addresses.
The client asks a recursive resolver, which uses cache or walks root → TLD → authoritative servers to get records like A/AAAA/CNAME.
TTL controls cache duration. In microservices and Kubernetes, DNS is also used for service discovery so we call services by name instead of hardcoding IPs."


🧠 Interview one-liner
“DNS is the internet’s phonebook: resolvers look up names via root/TLD/authoritative servers and cache answers according to TTL.”
